<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
    "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>

    <!--
        com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write
        ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
        Grants read/write access to files explicitly chosen by the user — including
        files sent via Apple Events (Finder double-click / "Open With"). Required for
        Hardened Runtime apps; without it, File operations silently fail for TCC-protected
        locations (Desktop, Documents, Downloads) even when the user initiated the open.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
        THE HARDENED RUNTIME SET, BELOW, IS INERT UNTIL codesign IS PASSED the runtime option.
        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        (Spelling it out rather than pasting the flag: a double hyphen is ILLEGAL inside an XML
        comment, `plutil -lint` accepts it anyway, and codesign's own stricter parser then refuses
        the whole file with "AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error near line 19" — which names a line,
        not a cause. Do not paste codesign flags into this file.)

        The bundle scripts add that flag only when signing with a real Developer ID identity
        (CRF_SIGN_IDENTITY), because notarisation REQUIRES the hardened runtime — and the hardened
        runtime is what breaks a .NET application unless the four keys below are present. An ad-hoc
        build is signed without it, so these keys change nothing there.

        They are declared here rather than "when we get round to notarising" because the failure
        they prevent is the expensive kind: the app notarises successfully, Gatekeeper is happy,
        and it then dies at launch or the first time it loads a device model. That is a round trip
        through Apple's notary service to discover.

        allow-jit + allow-unsigned-executable-memory
            .NET's JIT writes machine code into memory and then executes it. The hardened runtime
            forbids exactly that by default. Microsoft documents both as part of the default set
            for a .NET application.

        disable-library-validation
            Library validation refuses to load any library not signed by the same Team ID (or by
            Apple). circuitRF loads plenty that are neither: SkiaSharp's and HarfBuzz's natives,
            and — the one that matters — a vendor kit's own compiled device model, which
            osdi-worker dlopen()s at run time and which nobody has signed with our certificate.
            Without this key a kit reads correctly and refuses at Run, which is the exact failure
            packaging/macos/build-dmg.sh's worker check exists to prevent, arriving by another road.

        allow-dyld-environment-variables
            .NET sets DYLD_ variables for its own library resolution in some configurations.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>

</dict>
</plist>
